continually developing voice technology applications. For example, VAs are used by nutritionists in Thailand to provide biweekly meal plans (Namahoot et al., 2021). VAs are used in Russia to learn foreign languages (Al-Kaisi et al., 2021). Other well-known retail voice robots include Amazon’s Alexa, Google Assistant, and Apple’s Siri. Previous studies have reported that people use VAs mainly to seek information and make purchases, and for entertainment purposes (e.g., listening to music) (McLean & Osei-Frimpong, 2019; Purington et al., 2017; Rzepka, 2019; Sciuto et al., 2018). Studies have also found that consumers like to use VAs because they are convenient, easy, and enjoyable to use (Fernandes & Oliveira, 2021; Rzepka, 2019). Enjoyment is a particularly important aspect; studies on online shopping environments indicate that consumers who do not experience enjoyment during their shopping encounters are unlikely to use the service again (Martin et al., 2015). As it is projected that over eight billion VAs will be in use worldwide by 2023 and over 51% of users will apply them to online shopping (Laricchia, 2022), it is important to understand the role of enjoyment in the customer experience.
Despite their initial success on the market, many consumers still have concerns regarding VAs. Reasons include the unfamiliarity of talking with a machine and the fact that a machine is without emotions, as well as concerns over security and privacy (McCormick, 2017; Schwartz, 2019). To enhance the service quality of VAs, researchers have suggested giving VAs unique personalities (Zhou et al., 2019). A personality refers to the thinking patterns, emotions, behaviors, and psychological mechanisms that are unique to an individual (e.g., human, animal, or robot) (Funder, 2012). Personality is a controversial topic in the development of virtual agents because “it is not easy to explain why a robot should need a personality” (Moffat, 1997). However, researchers have found that using text, images, videos, and voice to anthropomorphize virtual agents can effectively improve the user experience (Waytz et al., 2014). In other words, equipping virtual agents with natural language and oral communication methods that are similar to those of humans makes them more acceptable to users. This includes giving virtual agents personality traits such as emotions, preferences, and specific thinking patterns (Afzal et al., 2019; Bawack et al., 2021; Zhou et al., 2019). As described by Cohen et al. (2004), it is natural for us to infer the personality of voices during human-to-human conversations, and we do so even if what we hear is pre-recorded and played through a virtual agent. Thus, in terms of psychology, even if users know that a virtual agent consists of a series of algorithms, they still respond positively to humanlike traits such as emotions, way of talking, or preferred vocabulary (Short, 2017). The development of personality thus influences the building of trust, which is a crucial factor influencing the usage of smart services (Garcia & Lopez, 2018). Compared to technologies such as blockchain, the diffusion of smart services based on semantic technology proceeds relatively slowly (Sun & Li, 2021; Viriyasitavat et al., 2022). Personalizing services to build trust could be an important route towards a better adoption rate (Bedué & Fritzsche, 2021). Thus, a major challenge in VA development is understanding how the personality of virtual agents influences willingness to use VAs. At present, VA-related research has mostly focused on technology (e.g., enhanced speech recognition abilities), security (e.g., increased privacy protection and personal information security), and user behavior (e.g., factors that encourage VA usage). Little research has been conducted on how VA personality influences user trust, perceived risk, and willingness to use. With personal VAs as the context, the authors therefore examined the influence of VA personality (i.e., humanlike traits and behavior traits) on perceived risk, perceived enjoyment, trust, attitude towards use, and continued usage intention.
RELATED WORKS
VA interactions are achieved via a voice-activated user interface. Users need only give simple voice commands, from which VAs extract key information via semantic analysis, carry out the task, and then report the results. VAs are currently widely applied in banking, tourism, and e-commerce. Research involving VAs has so far focused on the following three aspects.
