Technology
These studies have focused on strengthening VA capabilities (e.g., understanding human speech, producing voices similar to that of a real person, and adding emotional elements to the voices). For instance, Sermet and Demir (2021) introduced an open-source semantic web framework, Instant Expert, to help non-technical domain experts to build VAs for any web platform regardless of the underlying domain and technology. To improve semantic parsing, which is an important natural language processing (NLP) problem for VAs, Rongali et al. (2022) presented an automated methodology that utilizes modest amounts of unannotated data, which are typically easy to obtain. Kawase et al. (2020) employed a genetic algorithm to generate context-dependent parameters to enhance the accuracy of speech recognition. Other studies on enhancing VA capabilities include those by Park et al. (2018) and Sun (2020).
Security
This research has focused on detecting potential privacy and security issues and proposing solutions to enhance user trust. VAs have better “hearing” than human ears, and AI systems can detect many sounds that the human ear cannot. Yan et al. (2019) discovered that hackers may use ultrasonic sound waves that humans cannot perceive to issue malicious commands to VAs (e.g., Alexa and Microsoft Cortana) and steal personal information. Thapliyal et al. (2018) employed the Internet of Things and Amazon Echo to develop a VA system suitable for home use; this system has been verified as secure and expandable.
User Behavior
These studies have applied theoretical models from praxeology, psychology, and management to investigate the factors that influence willingness to use VAs (De Barcelos Silva et al., 2020). These studies focus on identifying important factors that influence why humans use VAs. For instance, McLean and Osei-Frimpong (2019) applied uses-and-gratification theory to find that personal and symbolic benefits are crucial factors underlying VA use, whereas the impact of hedonic benefits is small. Aside from personal benefits, the studies conducted by Nguyen et al. (2019) and Yang and Lee (2019) indicated that trust, perceived risk, perceived enjoyment, mobile self-efficacy, and perceived usefulness exert significant influence on willingness to use VAs.
In conclusion, existing studies have focused on in-depth investigations regarding aspects of technology, security, and user behavior. In terms of user behavior, the emphasis has been laid on factors influencing willingness to use VAs; little research has been conducted on human-computer interactions and VA personality. Nevertheless, an increasing number of studies have pointed out that personality will be a crucial direction for smart service development. VAs differ from chatbots, which simulate conversations using text messages. VAs interface through oral conversations, which are loaded with intuitive expressions and information. Importantly, voices contain a greater abundance of social cues than text, including personality, identity (e.g., gender and age), and emotions (e.g., amicability, happiness, and anger). On the whole, voice-based conversations offer a significant new direction for human-machine interactions.
