is a profitable strategy. VAs are an artificially intelligent service software whose voice outputs are humanized to reap the benefits of anthropomorphism. VA and human interactions feature many of the characteristics common in human-to-human interactions. Research has shown that when users perceive similarity between themselves and a non-human entity, they will rely more heavily on their knowledge of self to infer, understand, and make psychological projections (DiSalvo et al., 2002). Eyssel and Kuchenbrandt (2012) pointed out that as humans have a far better understanding of themselves than non-humans (e.g., voicebots), they perceive a higher level of risk associated with unfamiliar products (e.g., a product with a low degree of anthropomorphism). This psychological mechanism is a form of self-protection. In contrast, if a non-human entity is perceived as similar to humans, users will not be as wary of it and will be more willing to use it and share their private thoughts with it (Kiesler et al., 2008). Similarity with humans is not limited to appearance; any similarity in behavior or even social relationships can have a comparable effect. VAs imitate natural human pronunciation and tone, allowing them to “talk” with users. Talking is a crucial means of emotional exchange in human society, and only with enough communication can there be mutual understanding, trust, and enjoyment perceived during interactions. In conclusion, the authors formulated the following hypotheses:
H1a: The humanlike traits of VAs exert a negative impact on perceived risk. H1b: The humanlike traits of VAs exert a positive impact on perceived enjoyment. H1c: The humanlike traits of VAs exert a positive impact on trust. H1d: The humanlike traits of VAs exert a positive impact on attitude towards use.
Aside from the degree of anthropomorphism, the behavioral traits that users perceive during their conversations with the VAs may also exert a crucial influence. Research has shown that behavioral traits such as hand gestures, voice tones, and language are important social cues that people use to make inferences on the gender or personality of others to generate specific psychological impressions (e.g., like or dislike) (Isbister & Nass, 2000; Lee et al., 2006). In this study, the authors employed the widely used Google Assistant and Apple’s Siri as experiment tools. According to a study that surveyed 3,661 individuals between the ages of 18 and 65 in six countries (i.e., Argentina, Brazil, Chile, Spain, Germany, and the United Kingdom), Google Assistant and Apple’s Siri possess seven significant behavioral traits: practical, informed, up-to-date, well-mannered, logical, helpful, and kind (Garcia & Lopez, 2018). These behavioral traits are recognized as positive in human society. Research has indicated that in most cases, positive behavioral traits can significantly reduce the risk perceived by consumers and increase their willingness to use new technology. In interpersonal interactions, continued positive interactions also build a certain degree of trust and enjoyment. The authors therefore infer that the behavioral traits that VAs display during their interactions with users, including response speed, friendliness of wording, and quality of provided information, influence perceived risk, perceived enjoyment, trust, and attitude towards use. For example, Google Assistant allows users to use their usual vocabulary to make requests by speaking. Compared with inputting commands with a keyboard, using voice to conduct dialogue is more intuitive and convenient, which can effectively reduce user anxiety (Moreno & Mayer, 2007; Qiu & Benbasat, 2009). Based on the above friendly interaction model, users can quickly understand the benefits that applications such as VAs can bring to them; this can strengthen users’ trust in the system as well as their enjoyment in using it (Cheng et al., 2022; Natarajan & Gombolay, 2020). The authors therefore formulated the following hypotheses:
H2a: The behavioral traits of VAs exert a negative impact on perceived risk. H2b: The behavioral traits of VAs exert a positive impact on perceived enjoyment. H2c: The behavioral traits of VAs exert a positive impact on trust. H2d: The behavioral traits of VAs exert a positive impact on attitude towards use.
