Perceived risk refers to the unpredictable results or potential adverse circumstances that consumers perceive when purchasing a product or service; it is a subjective expected loss. Perceived risk generally has two important aspects: decision uncertainty (e.g., means of assessing the quality of a used car) and consequences of wrong decisions (e.g., whether the car will cause a family member to be injured because it is poor quality) (Featherman & Pavlou, 2003; Mitchell, 1999). In management, marketing, and consumer behavior, perceived risk has been found to be negatively correlated with product acceptance and trust and may include financial, psychological, social, private, safety, functional, and performance-related aspects (Bansal & Voyer, 2000; Benamati et al., 2021; Featherman & Pavlou, 2003; Park et al., 2019). VAs are a new type of technology, and most people are unfamiliar with the computational mechanisms of VAs, particularly with regard to personal privacy. When users feel that using VAs may pose significant risks, their trust in VAs will decline. The authors therefore formulated our third hypothesis as follows:
H3: Perceived risk exerts a negative influence on trust in VAs.
Perceived enjoyment is the degree of enjoyment that users experience during interactions with products or services. Researchers have indicated that intrinsic motivation plays a crucial role in the use of new technology. When users experience greater enjoyment, they will generate a positive attitude (Rouibah et al., 2021). Kim et al. (2013) discovered a positive relationship between perceived enjoyment and the quality of information system design: when users perceive that using a system is more interesting, they will rate its quality higher. The authors therefore propose our fourth hypothesis as follows:
H4: Perceived enjoyment exerts a positive influence on attitude towards using VAs.
Trust is an abstract and complex concept. Trust has been defined as “confidence in or reliance on some quality or attribute of a person or thing, or the truth of a statement” (McAllister et al., 2006; Simpson & Weiner, 2021). Within the context of technology adoption, Gefen (2002) explained trust as one party’s confidence that another party’s actions will meet their expectations. Specifically, the user trusts that the technical product is predictable, capable of accomplishing specified tasks, and acts with goodwill (McKnight et al., 2009). VAs are computer software (also known as a virtual agent) that receives and interprets instructions from the user and then retrieves the corresponding data from the database and feeds the data back to the user. If the database is not updated, the user will get the same answer by asking the same question. Thus, the predictability of VAs is high. Current mainstream VAs are able to complete tasks such as software function setting (e.g., alarm clock), schedule arrangement, information query (e.g., exchange rate), navigation, and purchasing goods (e.g., buying household supplies). They thus meet the needs of users in many daily activities. In terms of goodwill, as described previously, VAs such as Google Assistant and Apple’s Siri present well-mannered, helpful, and friendly personalities. Therefore, current mainstream VAs meet the conditions for trust. The positive personality traits of virtual agents in particular have been found to have a profound impact on consumer trust (Garcia & Lopez, 2018). The friendly and intuitive design of VAs means that users can quickly understand the benefits that VAs can bring to them; this further increases user trust in VAs as well as their enjoyment in using them (Cheng et al., 2022; Natarajan & Gombolay, 2020). Previous studies on online shopping have pointed out that trust is the foundation of the relationship between buyers and sellers. Basically, purchase intention is proportional to customer trust (Qalati et al., 2021; Zhao et al., 2020). Thus, in implementing innovative applications, such as mobile banking or e-government, building a foundation of trust is crucial to ensuring usage intentions (Lonkani et al., 2020; Rouibah et al., 2022). In other words, when users’ trust in VAs increases, their attitude towards using VAs should be more positive, and it will be easier for them to continue using VAs. Based on this discourse, the authors propose the fifth and sixth hypotheses as follows:
