H5: Trust exerts a positive influence on attitude towards using VAs. H6: Trust exerts a positive influence on intention to continue using VAs.
Attitude towards use refers to an individual’s subjectively assessed probability that a certain action will be taken, reflecting the individual’s willingness to adopt a certain product or service. Generally, an individual that has greater trust in a product or service will have a more positive attitude towards use. The technology acceptance model, which has widely been verified as highly reliable and valid, indicates that when people take a more positive attitude towards new technology, it will lead to a stronger intention to use said technology (Marangunić & Granić, 2015). Furthermore, attitude towards use has been demonstrated to be a crucial predictor of continued usage intention (Marangunić & Granić, 2015). In other words, a more positive attitude towards VA use indicates a greater willingness to continue to use VAs. The authors therefore formulated the following hypothesis:
H7: Attitude towards use of VAs exerts a positive influence on continued usage intention.
RESEARCH METHODOLOGY
Research Design
This study adopted the stimulus-organism-response (SOR) model (Mehrabian & Russell, 1974) and the theory of reasoned action (TRA) (Fishbein, 1979) to investigate the influence of VA personality on consumer trust and attitude towards VA use. According to the SOR model and the TRA, consumer behavior is triggered by various stimuli that originate from internal psychological and physiological factors (e.g., attitude towards VA or perceived enjoyment), subjective norms (e.g., social pressure from others), and the external environment (e.g., VA service quality). These stimuli strengthen the purchase motivation of consumers. In online shopping, the content (e.g., information and advertising) provided by VAs and their method of expression (i.e., VA personality) can change the emotional and cognitive state of customers, which then influences their attitude or behavioral responses (Chan et al., 2017). According to the TRA, the attitude of an individual refers to their positive or negative feelings and assessments towards engaging in certain behaviors (Ajzen, 1985; Fishbein, 1979). The authors thus established the research model of this study as shown in Figure 1. The authors referred to relevant literature for question items to measure each construct and adjusted them to fit our context. The operational definitions and source references of each construct are displayed in Table 1. For VA personality (i.e., humanlike traits and behavior traits), The authors referred to the ten question items developed by Lewis and Hardzinski (2015) and Garcia and Lopez (2018). Next, based on the consumer behavior studies conducted by Xu and Gupta (2009) and Lee et al. (2005), The authors adjusted the contents of the questionnaires to fit the overall perceptions of service quality (i.e., perceived risk and perceived enjoyment) that consumers have during their interactions with VAs, deriving seven question items. For trust, which measures the degree of trust that users have in the information content conveyed by VAs, the authors referred to the five question items designed by Dwyer et al. (2007) and Metzger (2004). Finally, for attitude towards use and continued usage intention, the authors respectively used three question items from the work of Xu et al. (2013) and Ahn et al. (2007). The resulting questionnaire contained a total of 28 question items all measured on a five-point Likert scale (1=strong disagreement to 5= strong agreement). The authors invited five domain experts and 20 consumers with VA experiences to participate in a pretest. After they completed the questionnaire, the authors asked them to offer suggestions on the wording of the question items. Based on their suggestions, the authors revised any ambiguous wording and removed statements and question items that seemed too similar. The Appendix displays the revised questionnaire.
