DISCUSSION AND IMPLICATIONS In this study, the authors developed an empirical approach based on the SOR model (Mehrabian and Russell, 1974) and the theory of reasoned action (Fishbein, 1979) to investigate how the personalities displayed by VAs stimulate consumers and generate varying degrees of changes in perceived risk, perceived enjoyment, attitude towards use, and trust, which in turn influence continued usage intention. In this section the authors discuss the empirical results, explain their contributions to theory and practice, and present the limitations of this study as well as directions for future research. Discussion of Findings First, the results of this study indicate that users derive more enjoyment from using VAs with more humanlike traits (e.g., appropriate tone and phrasing) and more positive behavioral traits (e.g., politeness and helpfulness). These factors also increase positive attitudes towards VA use. These results correspond with those of past studies (De Barcelos Silva et al., 2020). Perceived enjoyment is a crucial factor that determines whether users will ultimately continue using a product (Xu & Gupta, 2009). In consumer decisions, perceived enjoyment is a propelling force. When other conditions remain unchanged and the propelling force intensifies, the user will naturally be more willing to use a product or service. When individuals perceive enjoyment, their curiosity is aroused so that they continue interactions in the hope of deriving more satisfaction and pleasure. Therefore, individuals will be more willing to continue using VAs that satisfy their needs in enjoyable ways. In other words, VA personality reduces psychological impeding forces and strengthens propelling forces.
Second, regarding trust in VAs, the study results indicate that users have more trust in VAs with more humanlike and positive traits. This is an important finding for companies that are currently incorporating artificially intelligent voice-based services. At present, the reasoning mechanisms of a number of existing smart services are not disclosed, and they are often criticized as black-box operations, making users lose trust in smart services (e.g., concerns regarding data breaches and violations of personal rights); this is even more common among those who did not experience using
12

Journal of Global Information Management Volume 31 • Issue 1
smart services in their childhoods (Shin, 2021; Weitz et al., 2019). Research has shown that to enhance user trust in smart services, users must be able to understand, explain, and authenticate the underlying voice-processing technologies and decision frameworks of the services (Pedreschi et al., 2019). For this reason, a number of researchers have attempted to enhance the interpretability of smart services from the perspective of technology and design specifications (Kawase et al., 2020; Sun, 2020). This study provides decision-makers with another perspective: using more humanlike designs and giving VAs personalities (e.g., adding emotions, preferences, and certain ways of thinking) can also build user trust. If the VA does not behave like a human, users will find it difficult to predict the VA’s behavior based on their own experience. In contrast, VA anthropomorphism significantly reduces the psychological wariness of users. Importantly, this influence is intangible and does not have to be specifically explained to users. Successful anthropomorphism can significantly enhance user trust; this result should serve as good inspiration for corporate marketing and management decisions.
Third, the results of this study show that VA personality (i.e., humanlike traits and positive behavioral traits) has no significant effect on perceived risk. The authors believe that this may be because VA development is still in a stage of gradual growth, and some capabilities are immature. For example, although Apple’s Siri and Google Assistant can perform many daily tasks, they currently have limited ability to carry out in-depth conversations with users. Thus, there is less effective communication between users and VAs, which means interaction with them is unlikely to significantly reduce perceived risk as suggested by some studies (Kiesler et al., 2008). As VAs develop, and interactions become more humanlike, there is a possibility that the cognitive risk perceived by users may decrease. Furthermore, this study mainly measures the perceived risk associated with the use of personal information by VAs. At present, the protection of personal data is highly valued around the world, especially in developed countries. This may also explain the insignificant relationship between VA personality and perceived risk.
Finally, based on the results of this study, the authors suggest that companies already providing or preparing to develop VA services consider strengthening the following VA traits. First, behavior traits (e.g., friendliness, politeness, and enthusiasm) and trending functions (e.g., interesting methods of feedback or the addition of popular terms) can be designed for VAs based on the characteristics and preferences of the target users. Next, the degree of VA anthropomorphism (e.g., introducing humanlike traits such as pitch, softness of the voice, wording, and tone) can be increased. These characteristics will make users put more trust in VA services and have more positive attitudes towards using VAs, which will then influence their continued usage intention. From a practical perspective, the global market of VAs is rapidly increasing, due to the ever-growing use of smartphones, intelligent home appliances, customer service automation systems, and in-car entertainment systems. In fact, this industry is expected to grow from $13 billion in 2021 to $245 billion in 2030. At present, North America is the region with the largest VA market, while Asia is the region with the fastest growth in VA services (Straits, 2022). Our empirical results in this study have notable implications for the promotion of VAs in various markets, particularly in service industries that depend on interactions with a large number of customers. In these situations, the personification of VAs could help to gain customer trust and convey a sense of warmth, thereby increasing customer willingness to continue using the service.
Limitations and Future Research
Despite our efforts to maintain high levels of rigor and consistency in the research design, data collection, analysis, and result interpretation, this study remains subject to the following limitations associated with time and resource factors: (1) The thinking patterns and psychological mechanisms that a personality entails are complex. This study merely performed analyses based on the anthropomorphic traits and behavior traits of VAs, which may not be comprehensive. Directions for future research include personality traits such as gender, emotions, and sense of security. (2) With regard to perceived risk, this study only measured the risk that individuals perceive regarding the VAs themselves and
13

Journal of Global Information Management Volume 31 • Issue 1
did not include the information security awareness of the individuals, which may have caused some bias. Future studies could examine different risk factors, including psychological, social, and time risks. (3) This study only investigated two of the mainstream VAs (i.e., Google assistant and Apple’s Siri) on the market. Therefore, our results may not be applicable to other VA services such as the voice services of self-driving cars. Future studies could pursue research models for other types of VA services and compare a range of services to further understand the influence of VA personalities. (4) Trust is bidirectional: the personification of VAs can enhance users’ trust in them, and the higher the trust, the stronger their willingness to continue using VAs, while past studies have found that good user experiences enhance users’ trust in innovative technologies (Cui et al., 2022; Dutton & Shepherd, 2006; Gefen, 2000). In other words, when users have a certain degree of trust in VA due to anthropomorphism, their willingness to use it will increase; if they continue to interact with the VA and gain positive experiences, this trust may be further strengthened, increasing their willingness to use it again, forming a positive cycle. This issue is worthy of further empirical research to better understand the relationship between trust and willingness to use. CONFLICT OF INTEREST The authors declare that there are no conflicts of interest. FUNDING This work was supported with a grant from the Ministry of Science and Technology (MOST 111-2410-H-008-030-SSS), Taiwan, R.O.C.
